<sup><b>1</b></sup> In the Lord put I my trust: how say ye to my soul, Flee <i>as</i> a bird to your mountain?<br />
<sup><b>2</b></sup> For, lo, the wicked bend <i>their</i> bow, they make ready their arrow upon the string, that they may privily shoot at the upright in heart.<br />
<sup><b>3</b></sup> If the foundations be destroyed, what can the righteous do?<br />
<sup><b>4</b></sup> The Lord <i>is</i> in his holy temple, the Lord’s throne <i>is</i> in heaven: his eyes behold, his eyelids try, the children of men.<br />
<sup><b>5</b></sup> The Lord trieth the righteous: but the wicked and him that loveth violence his soul hateth.<br />
<sup><b>6</b></sup> Upon the wicked he shall rain snares, fire and brimstone, and an horrible tempest: <i>this shall be</i> the portion of their cup.<br />
<sup><b>7</b></sup> For the righteous Lord loveth righteousness; his countenance doth behold the upright. <br />
